<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samudera Pasai Sultanate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 13th century–<a href="/years/1524">1524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Samudra Pasai, Pasai Sultanate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Samudera Pasai Sultanate was a Muslim port kingdom on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhokseumawe">Lhokseumawe</a>, from the late 13th century. Its first known sultan, Malik al-Salih, died in 1297, making it one of the earliest Islamic states in maritime South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited it in the 1340s. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and gold port, it was occupied by the Portuguese in 1521 and absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> in 1524.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The sultanate is also known as Samudera, Pasai, Samudera Darussalam or Pacem. Little evidence survives for its study. Tradition, preserved in the 14th-century chronicle Hikayat Raja-raja Pasai, credits its foundation to Merah Silu, who later converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and took the name Malik ul Salih. The chronicle derives Samudera from a word for a very large ant that he is said to have found while hunting, and Pasai from the name of his hunting dog, Si-Pasai, which he buried there.</p><p>Arab and Indian Muslims had traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for many centuries, but substantial evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in Indonesia begins only in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> at the end of the 13th century, where two small Muslim trading kingdoms, Pasai and Peureulak or Perlak, existed. A royal tomb at Samudra, dated to 1297, is inscribed entirely in Arabic. The rise of such harbour kingdoms was probably commercial. With the collapse of Srivijayan power, traders were drawn to the northern Sumatran shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, safe from the pirate lairs at the southern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Moroccan traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited Samudra Pasai in 1345 on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and stayed about two weeks in its wooden-walled town as the sultan's guest. He noted that the ruler was a pious Muslim who followed the school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shafii">al-Shafi'i</a>, and that no territory east of Pasai was ruled by a Muslim. The sultan sent him on to China aboard one of his own junks.</p><p>By the end of the 14th century Samudra Pasai was a wealthy commercial centre. Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> had a hinterland rich in gold and forest produce, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> was being cultivated at the start of the 15th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> attacked and looted the place in the middle of the 14th century, and in the early 15th century the better protected harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> overtook it. The Portuguese occupied Pasai in 1521, ten years after taking Malacca. Their garrison left in 1524, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mughayat-syah">Ali Mughayat Syah</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, annexed the territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Historians have taken the tomb of Sultan Malik as-Saleh as a sign that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> had entered the Archipelago by the 13th century, although some think it may have come earlier. The stories of the Pasai kings are full of myths and legends, but they have helped to uncover the history of the kingdom. The University of Malikussaleh at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhokseumawe">Lhokseumawe</a> is named after the founder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samudera Pasai Sultanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aceh grew up at the northern tip of Sumatra, where the Strait of Malacca opens onto the Indian Ocean, an area earlier dominated by the port-kingdom of Lamuri and by <b>Samudera Pasai</b>, one of the first Muslim states of Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Southeast Asia Islam advanced through the port cities of the Malay world, where merchant communities and local rulers adopted it from the late thirteenth century onwards; the sultans of <b>Samudera Pasai</b> in Sumatra were among the first.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers were Muslim, following the example of nearby <b>Samudera Pasai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/pedir">Pedir</a></li></ul></section>
